<Base title="About the pilot"><article class="article"><p class="eyebrow">ABOUT / SCOPE</p><h1>A complete small investigation.</h1><p class="lede">The Open Questions Observatory design proposes a broad research catalog and carefully bounded experiments. This first pilot exercises one path with real literature and real observations.</p><h2>What was done</h2><p>An unanswered bounded question was selected through a documented search, its source motivation preserved, NOAA data acquired, a plan frozen, two models compared against a baseline, the analysis reproduced in restricted Linux containers, and a static searchable review site generated.</p><h2>What remains uncertain</h2><p>The literature search is limited and does not certify novelty. A preprint motivates the parent problem. The bounded experiment does not solve ENSO predictability. Few climate cycles, revised observations and omitted historical release delays limit the inference.</p><h2>Accountability and review</h2><p>Codex performed the source assessment, implementation, analysis and automated verification. The user approved public disclosure and accepted the report’s bounded negative interpretation. Independent scientific novelty and external expert review have not been established.</p><h2>Corrections</h2><p>Any later substantive correction should preserve the old result and append a dated notice. A failed reproduction, overlooked prior answer or data problem should change the visible assessment and remove unsupported claims.</p><h2>Data credit</h2><p>NOAA Climate Prediction Center; data provided by NOAA Physical Sciences Laboratory, Boulder, Colorado, USA. Source paper: Gan et al. (2026), arXiv:2601.02050v1, CC BY 4.0. No endorsement is implied.</p></article></Base>
